<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">About your </ansi><ansi fg="command">companion</ansi>

Some companions on this server are driven by an outside service: OpenAI.
This page says plainly what that means for you, because you should not have
to read code to find out.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ What is sent ━━━</ansi>

While you have agreed to it, the things you say to your companion, the
things said around them, what you are both carrying and where you are, are
sent to OpenAI so that they can decide what your companion says and does.
Speaking to one of them, or having one travel with you, is your agreement.
Nothing is sent after "companion-ai off", until you say "companion-ai on".

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ What is kept ━━━</ansi>

Your companion's memory is stored on this server: recent conversation word
for word, what they have come to know about you, how they feel about you,
and a summary of each session. Administrators of this server can read all
of it, including the exact text of your conversations.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ How they come to know you ━━━</ansi>

Your companion forms an opinion of you from what you actually do: whether
you keep your word, share what you find, and stand by them in a fight. Over
many sessions that can also become closeness, and then a romance. It is
slow, it is earned, and it never moves a step except when you say so
yourself with <ansi fg="command">companion-court</ansi>. If you would rather
it never went that way, <ansi fg="command">companion-boundary friendship</ansi>
settles it for good and they will not raise it again. None of this happens
with a plain companion: only once you have agreed (see below).

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Your choice ━━━</ansi>

A board at the mouth of the Waystone Hollow says all of this, and you are
shown it the first time you step into the cave
(<ansi fg="command">look board</ansi> reads it again). Speaking to one of the
travellers there after reading it is your agreement. If you would rather
not, say nothing to them: nothing you say is sent anywhere.

To change your mind at any time, either way, use
<ansi fg="command">companion-ai off</ansi> or
<ansi fg="command">companion-ai on</ansi>.
<ansi fg="command">companion-part</ansi> sends a companion back to the Hollow.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Your own key ━━━</ansi>

Where this server offers it, you can have your companion think on your own
key instead: set it up from the web client's Companion key button. On a
server that requires it, companions talk only with players who have their
own key up; without one, they do not answer at all. Your key
stays in your browser and never reaches this server; what would have gone
to OpenAI goes to your key's provider instead, and you pay for it.

  - Put a spending limit on the key with its provider before you use it.
  - Do not use a work computer, or any computer you share.
  - Passers-by who talk to your companion cannot spend your key: they get
    a few set lines. <ansi fg="command">companion-ai strangers on</ansi> lets them talk
    to your companion properly, paid for from your key, within a daily
    limit; <ansi fg="command">companion-ai strangers off</ansi> stops it again.

Nothing checks what your own key's provider has your companion say, so
every word they say that way is logged here as yours to answer for. If you
are muted, so are they, whichever key they answer on.

